# AGENTS.md — operating guide for AI coding agents

This project is `{{.project_name}}`, generated from an AAI Databricks bundle
template. The template name and version are recorded in `.aai-template.json`;
`CLAUDE.md` imports this file, so every agent works from one source of truth.
`README.md` explains the application itself; `make help` lists the workflow.

## Hard rules

1. **No secrets in Git.** Authentication is keyless (Azure identity and
   Databricks unified authentication). Configuration files carry identifiers
   and secret references, never secret values.
2. **Pull requests stay credential-free.** Never add credentials, OIDC
   permissions, or cloud login steps to the pull-request workflow.
3. **Logical names in code, physical names in configuration.** Endpoint,
   deployment, and index names belong in environment configuration, not in
   application code.
4. **Pin GitHub Actions to full commit SHAs** in any workflow you touch.

## The evaluation gate

`make evaluate` runs `evals/evaluate.py`. Its exit codes are a stable CI
contract: `0` pass, `2` threshold failed, `1` configuration or runtime
error. Scorers are versioned platform assets — select scorers and set
thresholds; never redefine one locally.

## What counts as a release

Code, model, prompt, tool, index, embedding, and chunking changes are all
application releases: each goes through the evaluation gate and the delivery
path in `docs/uat-promotion.md`.

## Where decisions go

Record non-obvious decisions — reversals, rejected alternatives, constraints
future work must not quietly undo — as dated files under `docs/decisions/`
(its README states the format). Never record secrets, personal data,
prompts, user content, or environment identifier values.
